# Paperless-ngx stack secrets (services VM) — app, admin, Postgres
# Copy to .sops.env and encrypt with:
#   sops -e -i .sops.env
#
# Key-set contract: this file, the real .sops.env and the test fixture
# tests/fixtures/paperless.sops.env must all carry EXACTLY the same keys — the
# stack-env-drift lint fails on any difference.
#
# Reminder from CLAUDE.md: any value containing '$' must be single-quoted in
# the plaintext before encrypting — the decrypt-and-source path expands it
# otherwise. (None of the generate commands below emit '$'.)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Django's signing key. Paperless does NOT crash without it: it falls back to
# the literal default that ships in the public repository, so every session
# cookie and password-reset token in the fleet would be forgeable by anyone
# who can read GitHub.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
PAPERLESS_SECRET_KEY=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The superuser paperless creates on first start (PAPERLESS_ADMIN_USER /
# _PASSWORD are read once, at initial migration). Single token on purpose — a
# space would need quoting to survive the decrypt-and-source path AND
# compose's env_file parsing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
PAPERLESS_ADMIN_USER=admin
PAPERLESS_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme_strong_password

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL password, TWICE with the SAME value, exactly like immich and
# firefly: the postgres image reads POSTGRES_PASSWORD to initialise the role,
# paperless-ngx connects with PAPERLESS_DBPASS, and `env_file` hands both
# containers this whole .env. If the two ever differ the app cannot
# authenticate to its own database.
#
# 🚨 POSTGRES_PASSWORD is applied by initdb ONLY — on FIRST start, against an
# empty /mnt/fast/paperless/pgdata. Changing it later does nothing to the
# existing role and the app just starts failing to authenticate. To rotate on
# a cluster that already exists, change BOTH keys here and then run:
#   docker exec -it paperless_db psql -U paperless \
#     -c "ALTER USER paperless PASSWORD '<new value>';"
#
# ⚠ These two lived hardcoded as the literal `paperless` in compose.yaml until
# 2026-08-31. If this cluster was ever started before that, the role's
# password on disk is still `paperless` — do the ALTER USER above.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 24
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_24
PAPERLESS_DBPASS=changeme_same_value_as_POSTGRES_PASSWORD
